ection because it lies in both intervals." 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186063974 1074 "We can start by sketching the two intervals $(-\\infty, 3]$ and $(0, \\infty)$ separately:\n\n![](images/25/255da904e7d271006cef6a272379b8f160fc6cc9e5f8ede39d91141aea2eff7b.png)\n\n![](images/31/31ad28b45aa2cefa4d83cc06710e9ae81446809ec0ac42da5baae36e235e35a9.png)\n\nThe intersection $(-\\infty, 3] \\cap (0, \\infty)$ represents the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/6a/6a92713b9964c9b1583622553a7cdb3c9c700ecad95cc0581f1538d18d1fa42a.png)\n\nThus, $(-\\infty, 3]\\cap(0, \\infty)={\\color{blue}(0,3]}.$ This corresponds to the inequality\n\n$$\n0 < x \\leq 3.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The point $0$ is not included in the intersection since it does not lie in the second interval. On the other hand, the point $3$ is included in the intersection because it lies in both intervals." 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186063976 1074 "We start by sketching the solutions to the two inequalities separately:\n\n![](images/16/166ce99c0ceedf97d38066b544b5dc6ff7ca63296572d952536193a83a410d94.png)\n\n![](images/7f/7ff88166ac6f9e631cf8928e91c1c299986893051013f15387e153ecf4237866.png)\n\nThe common set of values consists of the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/a7/a77508881158e793810249877b3941fe52ae9e04056813d2222daae4aa0d5e50.png)\n\nTherefore, the common set of values is\n${\\color{blue}(-\\infty, -1]}$." 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186063976 1074 "We start by sketching the solutions to the two inequalities separately:\n\n![](images/16/166ce99c0ceedf97d38066b544b5dc6ff7ca63296572d952536193a83a410d94.png)\n\n![](images/7f/7ff88166ac6f9e631cf8928e91c1c299986893051013f15387e153ecf4237866.png)\n\nThe common set of values consists of the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/a7/a77508881158e793810249877b3941fe52ae9e04056813d2222daae4aa0d5e50.png)\n\nTherefore, the common set of values is\n${\\color{blue}(-\\infty, -1]}.$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186063977 1071 "Given the inequalities $x < 1$, $x \\geq 0$, and $-2 < x < 4$, express their common set of solutions as an interval." 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186063977 1071 "Given the inequalities $x < 1,$  $x \\geq  0,$ and $-2 < x < 4,$ express their common set of solutions as an interval." 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186063977 1074 "To find the common set of values, we graph the inequalities on a number line and find where they overlap:\n\n![](images/25/2544eed03d4349fdd897e62447a9566843c53c7ab24001525d5148dba14fecd4.png)\n\nTherefore, the common set of values is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\left[0, 1\\right)}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The point $0$ is included in the intersection since it lies in all three intervals. On the other hand, the point $1$ is not included in the intersection because it does not lie in the first interval." 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186063977 1074 "To find the common set of values, we graph the inequalities on a number line and find where they overlap:\n\n![](images/25/2544eed03d4349fdd897e62447a9566843c53c7ab24001525d5148dba14fecd4.png)\n\nTherefore, the common set of values is\n${\\color{blue}\\left[0, 1\\right)}.$\n\n**Watch out!** The point $0$ is included in the intersection since it lies in all three intervals. On the other hand, the point $1$ is not included in the intersection because it does not lie in the first interval." 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186064173 1038 17592186505737 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186064173 1038 17592186505795 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186064174 1038 17592186505741 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186064174 1038 17592186505800 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186064175 103