6045417 false] [17592186064669 1071 "Given the inequalities $x\\ge -6,$ $-3<x<5,$ and  $x\\le 3,$ express their common set of solutions as an interval." 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186064669 1074 "To find the common set of values, we graph the inequalities on a number line and find where they overlap:\n\n![](images/d6/d68477510b8339f48eebe5105dd081661e0c299a89712662b6f13a7e15c7b2e7.png)\n\nTherefore, the common set of values is\n$(-3,3].$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186064702 1070 17592186045485 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186064702 1071 "Which of the following inequalities corresponds to the intersection $(- ∞, 0)∩(-4, ∞)∩(-4, 0]$?" 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186064702 1071 "Which of the following inequalities corresponds to the intersection $(-\\infty ,0)\\cap (-4,\\infty )\\cap (-4,0]?$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186064702 1074 "To find the intersection of the intervals, we graph the separate intervals on a number line and find where they overlap:\n\n![](images/67/675114239d8d7f94928fa7914d23bb9fb81579624ae8a392aa9fde8b2df643cd.png)\n\nThus, $(-\\infty ,0)\\cap (-4,\\infty )\\cap (-4,0]=(-4,0).$ This corresponds to the inequality\n\n$$\n-4<x<0.\n$$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186065436 1064 "The **intersection** of two intervals is their set of common values, and is denoted by the intersection symbol $\\cap$.\n\nFor example, the intersection of the two intervals $(-\\infty, 2]$ and $[-1,\\infty)$ is denoted\n\n$$\n(-\\infty, 2] \\cap [-1,\\infty)\n$$\n\nand corresponds to the set of values that are in both intervals $(-\\infty, 2]$ and $[-1,\\infty)$.\n\nTo represent the intersection on a number line, we can start by sketching the two intervals separately:\n\n![](images/a0/a0b486ac0f981fc5a0161c5eabba9ec257bd0a03e5f0e27397f6fe8e81eb39a5.png)\n\n![](images/8a/8a98f83055b49c14ed08fff658ace9527c5134879a66245aff02e446d44a5c87.png)\n\nThe intersection $(-\\infty, 2] \\cap [-1,\\infty)$ represents the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/f7/f7a5126928d43be82d3e4a167b52b34d8ed4ec33e94b991ecb8486caf3e3d4a9.png)\n\nTherefore, the intersection of the intervals is ${\\color{blue}[-1,2]}$. Symbolically, we write\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-\\infty,2]} \\cap {\\color{red}[-1,\\infty)} = {\\color{blue}[-1,2]}\n$$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186065436 1064 "The **intersection** of two intervals is their set of common values, and is denoted by the intersection symbol $\\cap.$\n\nFor example, the intersection of the two intervals $(-\\infty, 2]$ and $[-1,\\infty)$ is denoted\n\n$$\n(-\\infty, 2] \\cap [-1,\\infty)\n$$\n\nand corresponds to the set of values that are in both intervals $(-\\infty, 2]$ and $[-1,\\infty).$\n\nTo represent the intersection on a number line, we can start by sketching the two intervals separately:\n\n![](images/a0/a0b486ac0f981fc5a0161c5eabba9ec257bd0a03e5f0e27397f6fe8e81eb39a5.png)\n\n![](images/8a/8a98f83055b49c14ed08fff658ace9527c5134879a66245aff02e446d44a5c87.png)\n\nThe intersection $(-\\infty, 2] \\cap [-1,\\infty)$ represents the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/f7/f7a5126928d43be82d3e4a167b52b34d8ed4ec33e94b991ecb8486caf3e3d4a9.png)\n\nTherefore, the intersection of the intervals is ${\\color{blue}[-1,2]}.$ Symbolically, we write\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-\\infty,2]} \\cap {\\color{red}[-1,\\infty)} = {\\color{blue}[-1,2]}.\n$$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186067294 1075 17592186067295 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186067294 1075 17592186067296 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186067294 1075 17592186067297 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186067294 1075 17592186067299 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186067294 1075 17592186505133 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186067294 1075 1759218650518