87891 1050 420.0 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186088265 1070 17592186045487 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186088265 1074 "The function $f(x)$ above is defined for values between $x=1$ and $x=4.$\n\n- Since there is a closed circle at $x=1,$ this point is included in the domain.\n\n- On the other hand, because there is an open circle at $x=4,$ this point is not included in the domain.\n\nTherefore, the domain of this function is\n\n$$\n1\\le x<4.\n$$\n\nUsing interval notation, we write this as\n\n$$\nx\\in [1,4).\n$$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186088266 1070 17592186045485 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186088266 1074 "The function above is defined for values between $x=1$ and $x=4.$\n\n- Since there is a closed circle at $x=1,$ this point is included in the domain.\n\n- Since there is a closed circle at $x=4,$ this point is also included in the domain.\n\nTherefore, the domain of this function is\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 4.\n$$\n\nUsing interval notation, we can write this as\n\n$$\nx\\in [1,4].\n$$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186088865 1064 "Consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x\n$$\n\nshown below.\n\n![](images/a2/a2660977b21e476e1a295e215ac22888625763b12b1685521c289adf8326f78c.png)\n\nNo matter which number $x$ we pick as an input, $f(x)$ always will return an output. Therefore, we can write the domain of this function as\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$\n\nLet's compare this to the function\n\n$$\ng(x) = \\dfrac 1 x\n$$\n\nshown below:\n\n![](images/84/84fefed3d54e57467ca3ebcf823c6bbf5c3c6aacce2f7542230b1abacfd97f46.png)\n\nWe cannot substitute the value $x=0$ into the function $g(x)$ because division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $x=0$ is not part of the domain of $g(x)$. However, we can use any other number $x$ as input for $g(x)$. Therefore, the domain of $g(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty, 0)\\cup (0,\\infty)\n$$\n\nThis notation means that any number *apart from zero* can be used as an input for $g(x)$." 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186088865 1064 "Consider the function $f(x)=2x,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/a2/a2660977b21e476e1a295e215ac22888625763b12b1685521c289adf8326f78c.png)\n\nNo matter which number $x$ we pick as an input, $f(x)$ always will return an output. Therefore, we can write the domain of this function as \n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nLet's compare this to the function $g(x) = \\dfrac 1 x,$ shown below:\n\n![](images/84/84fefed3d54e57467ca3ebcf823c6bbf5c3c6aacce2f7542230b1abacfd97f46.png)\n\nWe cannot substitute the value $x=0$ into the function $g(x)$ because division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $x=0$ is not part of the domain of $g(x).$ However, we can use any other number $x$ as input for $g(x).$ Therefore, the domain of $g(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty, 0)\\cup (0,\\infty).\n$$\n\nThis notation means that any number *apart from zero* can be used as an input for $g(x).$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186088866 1064 "Let's consider the function $f(x)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/2a/2a666cbc79869f13f7079db92396ae6f7ad2b2041b2aa0ea704149119a850eed.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The function is not defined for $x<0$, nor is it defined for $x > 2$.\n- The function has filled circles at $x=0$ and $x=2$, so it is defined at both of these points.\n- In addition, the function is defined for every value of $x$ between $0$ and $2$.\n\nTherefore, the domain of this function is\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq 2\n$$\n\nWe can also express the domain using interval notation:\n\n$$\nx\\in [0,2]\n$$\n\nThe square brackets $[\\:]$ indicate that the endpoints $x=0$ and $x=2$ are included in the domain." 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186088866 1064 "Let's consider the function $f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/2a/2a666cbc79869f13f7079db92396ae6f7ad2b2041b2aa0ea704149119a850eed.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The function is not defined for $x<0,$ nor is it defined for $x > 2.$\n\n- The function has filled circles at $x=0$ and $x=2,$ so it is defined at both of these points.\n\n- In addition, the function is defined for every value of $x$ between $0$ and $2.$\n\nTherefore, the domain of this function is\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq 2.\n$$\n\nWe can also express the domain using interval notation:\n\n$$\nx\\in [0,2]\n$$\n\nThe square brackets $[\\:]$ indicate that the endpoints $x=0$ and $x=2$ are included in the domain." 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186088981 1064 "The **domain** of a function is the set of all input values for which it is defined.\n\nFor example, consider the function represented by the mapping diagram below.\n\n![](images/f7/f7c39c6cdd67f1b69248b38e009b6de12acef250a59543aca2d2922ec1801d57.png)\n\n \n\nThe domain corresponds to the set of values in the \"Input\" set. Therefore, the domain of this function is\n\n$$\n\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's now consider a situation where a function is defined using a table." 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186088981 1064 "The **domain** of a function is the set of all input values for which it is defined.\n\nFor example, consider the function represented by the mapping diagram below.\n\n![](images/f7/f7c39c6cdd67f1b69248b38e009b6de12acef250a59543aca2d2922ec1801d57.png)\n\nThe domain corresponds to the set of values in the \"Input\" set. Therefore, the domain of this function is\n\n$$\n\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider a situation where a function is defined using a table." 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186088982 1064 "Now consider the function $y=g(x)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/c9/c99d3cbb1b7ba516cc2cf75856aeb17b9f91d23185d6fd25319a492ca4e3b164.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The function is not defined for $x<0$, nor is it defined for $x > 2$.\n- The function has open circles at $x=0$ and $x=2$, so it is *not* defined at either of these points.\n- However, the function is defined for every value of $x$ between $0$ and $2$.\n\nTherefore, the domain of this function is\n\