ivative of $f(x)$, is given above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $8, 5$ and $6$, respectively (from left to right). Find $f(4)$ if $f(-4) =-1$." 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186101544 1071 "![](images/88/881ec029bea9d85b0f1f76c3c62ca0343b9b6103c57122f6dc06d02cf5b30d8c.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on the interval $[-4,4].$ The graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is given above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $8,5$ and $6,$ respectively (from left to right). Find $f(4)$ if $f(-4)=-1.$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186101544 1074 "From the fundamental theorem of calculus, we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(4)-f(-4) \\\\ {\\int }_{-4}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(4)-(-1) \\\\ f(4) & =-1+{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, remember that the integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where we take the area as negative if the curve lies below the $x$-axis. So, using the labels on the graph, the integral is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x=8+(-5)+6=9.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the value of the integral, we can find $f(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =-1+{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+9 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186102004 1064 "Suppose we are given the graph of the function $f(x)$, as shown below. The function has critical points at $x=-1$, $x=2$, and an inflection point at $x=0.5$.\nHow can we sketch the graph of the derivative $f'(x)$?\n\n![](images/b3/b3fcc7a08d2a083567cc2de62c9bb0c6e9516552c8a2f08d2e814604f5ec84f8.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have $f'(x)=0$.\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have $f'(x) > 0$.\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have $f'(x) < 0$.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nUsing this information, we can identify the regions where $f'(x)$ should cross the $x$-axis. According to the table, these are points $(-1,0)$ and $(2,0)$:\n\n![](images/e9/e98d21084e06d2a28509b0b68f449c5f42959ee1adad9b3dbf912885968a3b33.png)\n\nAdditionally, we are told that the function has an inflection point at $x = 0.5$. So $f''(0.5)=0$ and the tangent to the graph of $y=f'(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of $f'(0.5)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x)$, we can conclude that at $x = 0.5$ the slope is negative. Therefore, we get a point below the $x$-axis:\n\n![](images/59/5994a9139fbd1c530692a2fb40b2fb1b4b7bd0b34b2bf7ff7194390542748977.png)\n\nFinally, connecting up the parts of the graph above, we get an approximate sketch of $f'(x)$:\n\n![](images/f2/f21fca7744d7196e457f0a9d4511f644a3cf1e8c61018c152fc74ea7f6999f3e.png)" 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186102004 1064 "Suppose we are given the graph of the function $f(x),$ as shown below. The function has critical points at $x=-1,$ $x=2,$ and an inflection point at $x=0.5.$\nHow can we sketch the graph of the derivative $f'(x)?$\n\n![](images/b3/b3fcc7a08d2a083567cc2de62c9bb0c6e9516552c8a2f08d2e814604f5ec84f8.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have $f'(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have $f'(x) > 0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have $f'(x) < 0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nUsing this information, we can identify the regions where $f'(x)$ should cross the $x$-axis. According to the table, these are points $(-1,0)$ and $(2,0)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/e9/e98d21084e06d2a28509b0b68f449c5f42959ee1adad9b3dbf912885968a3b33.png)\n\nAdditionally, we are told that the function has an inflection point at $x = 0.5.$ So $f''(0.5)=0$ and the tangent to the graph of $y=f'(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of $f'(0.5)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that at $x = 0.5$ the slope is negative. Therefore, we get a point below the $x$-axis:\n\n![](images/59/5994a9139fbd1c530692a2fb40b2fb1b4b7bd0b34b2bf7ff7194390542748977.png)\n\nFinally, connecting up the parts of the graph above, we get an approximate sketch of $f'(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/f2/f21fca7744d7196e457f0a9d4511f644a3cf1e8c61018c152fc74ea7f6999f3e.png)" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186102019 1064 "Suppose the function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on the interval $[0,5]$. The graph of $y=f'(x)$, which consists of two line segments, is shown below. Additionally, we know that ${f(0)=2}$. How can we find, for example, the value of $f(4)$?\n\n![](images/fe/feb899a5b4728254a153d40194220c5237eb4ef84a537e22c094cbf1639ab3c2.png)\n\nThe fundamental theorem of calculus states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{4} f'(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x = f(4) - f(0)\n$$\n\nand we know that\n\n$$\nf(0)=2\n$$\n\nso we can find $f(4)$ if we can figure out the value of the integral.\n\nRemember that the integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis. To compute the area under the curve, let's split up the integral into two integrals which represent areas that are easier to compute:\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{4} f'(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = \\underbrace{\\int_{0}^{2} f'(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{A_1} + \\underbrace{\\int_{2}^{4} f'(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{A_2}\n$$\n\nThe two corresponding areas $A_1$ and $A_2$ shown in the graph below:\n\n![](images/1f/1feed4138e13b671489583ec10658b377e578d50a25a3d6be5a19d8c22d85706.png)\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [0,2]$. The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{1} &= \\frac{1}{2}(a + b)h \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (1 + 2) \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [2,4]$. The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{2} &= ab \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of the original integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{4}f^{′}(x)dx &= A_{1} + A_{2} \\\\\n&= 3 + 4 \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the value above and the fact that\n\n$$\nf(0)=2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{4}f^{′}(x)dx &= f(4) - f(0) \\\\\n7 &= f(4) - 2 \\\\\nf(4) &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186102019 1064 "Suppose the function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on the interval $[0,5].$ The graph of $y=f'(x),$ which consists of two line segments, is shown below. Additionally, we know that ${f(0)=2}.$ How can we find, for example, the value of $f(4)?$\n\n![](images/fe/feb899a5b4728254a153d40194220c5237eb4ef84a537e22c094cbf1639ab3c2.png)\n\nThe fundamental theorem of calculus states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{4} f'(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x = f(4) - f(0),\n$$\n\nand we know that $f(0)=2,$ so we can find $f(4)$ if we can figure out the value of the integral. \n\nRemember that the integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis. To compute the area under the curve, let's split up the integral into two integrals which represent areas that are easier to compute:\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{4} f'(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = \\underbrace{\\int_{0}^{2} f'(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{A_1} + \\underbrace{\\int_{2}^{4} f'(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{A_2}.\n$$\n\nThe two corresponding areas $A_1$ and $A_2$ shown in the graph below:\n\n![](images/1f/1feed4138e13b671489583ec10658b377e578d50a25a3d6be5a19d8c22d85706.png)\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [0,2].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1+2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [2,4].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =ab \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of the original integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =3+4 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the value above and the fact that $f(0)=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(4)-f(0) \\\\ 7 & =f(4)-2 \\\\ f(4) & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186105525 1074 "Since two sides are congruent, $\\overline{BC} \\cong \\overline{AB}$, we conclude that $\\triangle ABC$ is isosceles. Consequently, the base angles must have the same measure:\n\n$$\nm\\angle C = m\\angle A = x -10^\\circ\n$$\n\nNow, since the internal angles of any triangle sum to $180^\\circ$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A + m∠B + m∠C &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n(x - 10^{∘}) + (x + 20^{∘}) + (x - 10^{∘}) &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n3x &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 60^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=60^\\circ$ into the equation for the measure of $\\angle A$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A &= x - 10^{∘} \\\\\n&= 60^{∘} - 10^{∘} \\\\\n&= 50^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo\n\n$$\nm\\angle A = 50^\\circ\n$$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186105525 1074 "Since two sides are congruent, $\\overline{BC} \\cong \\overline{AB},$ we conclude that $\\triangle ABC$ is isosceles. Consequently, the base angles must have the same measure:\n\n$$\nm\\angle C = m\\angle A = x -10^\\circ\n$$\n\nNow, since the internal angles of any triangle sum to $180^\\circ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ (x-{10}^{∘})+(x+{20}^{∘})+(x-{10}^{∘}) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={60}^{∘}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=60^\\circ$ into the equation for the measure of $\\angle A$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A & =x-{10}^{∘} \\\\  & ={60}^{∘}-{10}^{∘} \\\\  & ={50}^{∘}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $m\\angle A = 50^\\circ.$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186105526 1074 "First, from the diagram, notice that $\\angle{HFG}$ and $\\angle{EFH}$ are supplementary. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠HFG + m∠EFH &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠HFG + 105^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠HFG &= 75^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\triangle{FGH}$ is an isosceles triangle where $\\overline{GH} \\cong \\overline{FH}$, the base angles must be equal:\n\n$$\nm\\angle{G} = m\\angle{HFG}=75^\\circ\n$$\n\nFinally, since the interior angles of any triangle sum to $180^\\circ$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠G + m∠HFG + m∠H &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n75^{∘} + 75^{∘} + m∠H &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n150^{∘} + m∠H &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠H &= 30^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186105526 1074 "First, from the diagram, notice that $\\angle{HFG}$ and $\\angle{EFH}$ are supplementary. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠HFG+m∠EFH & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HFG+{105}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HFG & ={75}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\triangle{FGH}$ is an isosceles triangle where $\\overline{GH} \\cong \\overline{FH}$, the base angles must be equal:\n\n$$\nm\\angle{G} = m\\angle{HFG}=75^\\circ.\n$$\n\nFinally, since the interior angles of any triangle sum to $180^\\circ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠G+m∠HFG+m∠H & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {75}^{∘}+{75}^{∘}+m∠H & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {150}^{∘}+m∠H & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠H & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186105527 1071 "In the figure below, $m\\angle{CAD}=100^\\circ$. What is the measure of $\\angle{D}$?\n\n![](images/59/59bb54f508b8045eeffb7cdbf547b6be0f074009b3288fb13e115159909782a8.png)" 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186105527 1071 "In the figure below, $m\\angle{CAD}=100^\\circ.$ What is the measure of $\\angle{D}?$\n\n![](images/59/59bb54f508b8045eeffb7cdbf547b6be0f074009b3288fb13e115159909782a8.png)" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186105527 1074 "First, notice that $\\triangle{ABC}$ is an equilateral triangle. Consequently, all of the interior angles must be equal to $60^\\circ$:\n\n$$\nm\\angle{BAC}=m\\angle{ABC}=m\\angle{C}=60^\\circ\n$$\n\nAlso, since $\\triangle{ADB}$ is an isosceles triangle, the base angles must have equal measure:\n\n$$\nm\\angle{BAD}=m\\angle{ABD}\n$$\n\n![](images/a9/a997837f549d59b2a554bf6bc60d9de3ebf6b4b97188ac3d0f07342fb4af6507.png)\n\nNow, since we're told that\n\n$$\nm\\angle{CAD}=100^\\circ\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠BAD &= m∠CAD - m∠BAC \\\\\n&= 100^{∘} - 60^{∘} \\\\\n&= 40^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in $\\triangle{BAD}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠BAD + m∠D + m∠ABD &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n2m∠BAD + m∠D &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n2 \\cdot 40^{∘} + m∠D &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n80^{∘} + m∠D &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠D &= 100^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186105527 1074 "First, notice that $\\triangle{ABC}$ is an equilateral triangle. Consequently, all of the interior angles must be equal to $60^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\nm\\angle{BAC}=m\\angle{ABC}=m\\angle{C}=60^\\circ\n$$\n\nAlso, since $\\triangle{ADB}$ is an isosceles triangle, the base angles must have equal measure:\n\n$$\nm\\angle{BAD}=m\\angle{ABD}\n$$\n\n![](images/a9/a997837f549d59b2a554bf6bc60d9de3ebf6b4b97188ac3d0f07342fb4af6507.png)\n\nNow, since we're told that $m\\angle{CAD}=100^\\circ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BAD & =m∠CAD-m∠BAC \\\\  & ={100}^{∘}-{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={40}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in $\\triangle{BAD}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BAD+m∠D+m∠ABD & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2m∠BAD+m∠D & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2\\cdot {40}^{∘}+m∠D & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {80}^{∘}+m∠D & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠D & ={100}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186105584 1074 "Since $\\triangle ABC$ is an isosceles triangle with base $\\overline{AB}$, we have that $\\overline{BC} \\cong \\overline{AC}$. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nBC &= AC \\\\\n4x - 3 &= 2x + 1 \\\\\n2x &= 4 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into the expression for the measure of $\\overline{AB}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= x + 4 \\\\\n&= (2) + 4 \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AB=6$." 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186105584 1074 "Since $\\triangle ABC$ is an isosceles triangle with base $\\overline{AB}$, we have that $\\overline{BC} \\cong \\overline{AC}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =AC \\\\ 4x-3 & =2x+1 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into the expression for the measure of $\\overline{AB}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =x+4 \\\\  & =(2)+4 \\\\  & =6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AB=6.$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186105738 1038 17592186505752 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186105738 1038 17592186505835 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186105739 1038 17592186505731 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186105739 1038 17592186505815 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186105740 1038 17592186505797 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186105740 1038 17592186505818 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186105741 1038 17592186505742 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186105741 1038 17592186505837 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186106021 1050 780.0 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186107043 1064 "Remember that longer sides in a triangle are opposite to larger angles, and vice versa. But what if two (or more) sides have the same length?\n\nAs you might have guessed, if two sides have the same length, then the opposite angles have the same measure. And vice-versa: if two angles have the same measure, then the opposite sides have the same length.\n\nSo, we obtain the following important properties of isosceles and equilateral triangles:\n\n- A triangle is **equilateral** if and only if all of its angles are of $60^\\circ$.\n\n![](images/ad/ad56532db83ae54eeabf04d5a182a850bed14d9d0a65518b8048c3c0c112c371.png)\n\n- A triangle is **isosceles** if and only if it has two congruent angles.\n\n![](images/2b/2b5d6779527eae10e108ffcdc80745f63f3f38e41474673990bf74c83d2e6a64.png)\n\n**Note:** The statement above encompasses two theorems:\n\n- The **isosceles triangle theorem**:*If a triangle is isosceles, then the base angles are congruent.*\n- The **converse isosceles triangle theorem**: *If two angles in a triangle have the same measure, then the triangle must be isosceles.*" 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186107043 1064 "Remember that longer sides in a triangle are opposite to larger angles, and vice versa. But what if two (or more) sides have the same length?\n\nAs you might have guessed, if two sides have the same length, then the opposite angles have the same measure. And vice-versa: if two angles have the same measure, then the opposite sides have the same length.\n\nSo, we obtain the following important properties of isosceles and equilateral triangles:\n\n- A triangle is **equilateral** if and only if all of its angles are of $60^\\circ.$\n\n![](images/ad/ad56532db83ae54eeabf04d5a182a850bed14d9d0a65518b8048c3c0c112c371.png)\n\n- A triangle is **isosceles** if and only if it has two congruent angles.\n\n![](images/2b/2b5d6779527eae10e108ffcdc80745f63f3f38e41474673990bf74c83d2e6a64.png)\n\n**Note:** The statement above encompasses two theorems:\n\n- The **isosceles triangle theorem**:\n\n*If a triangle is isosceles, then the base angles are congruent.*\n\n- The **converse isosceles triangle theorem**: \n\n*If two angles in a triangle have the same measure, then the triangle must be isosceles.*" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186110400 1074 "*Method 1*\n\nLet's start by finding the value of the missing interior angle at $B$. We use the fact that the interior and exterior angles are supplementary:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n73^{∘} + m∠B &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠B &= 180^{∘} - 73^{∘} \\\\\nm∠B &= 107^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $x$ using the fact that the interior angles of a triangle sum to $180^\\circ$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A + m∠B + m∠C &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n41^{∘} + 107^{∘} + 2x &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n148^{∘} + 2x &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n2x &= 32^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 16^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Method 2*\n\nWe could rewrite the equation\n\n$$\nm\\angle{A} +m\\angle{B} +m\\angle{C} = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nas\n\n$$\n180^{∘} - m∠B_(⏟)_(Exterior angle at [math]B[/math]) = m∠A + m∠C\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we can solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n180^{∘} - m∠B_(⏟)_(Exterior angle at [math]B[/math]) &= m∠A + m∠C \\\\\n73^{∘} &= 41^{∘} + 2x \\\\\n32^{∘} &= 2x \\\\\n16^{∘} &= x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186110400 1074 "*Method 1*\n\nLet's start by finding the value of the missing interior angle at $B.$ We use the fact that the interior and exterior angles are supplementary:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{73}^{∘}+m∠B & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & ={180}^{∘}-{73}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & ={107}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $x$ using the fact that the interior angles of a triangle sum to $180^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {41}^{∘}+{107}^{∘}+2x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {148}^{∘}+2x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & ={32}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={16}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\n*Method 2*\n\nWe could rewrite the equation $m\\angle{A} +m\\angle{B} +m\\angle{C} = 180^\\circ$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{{180}^{∘}-m∠B}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Exterior angle at [math]B[/math]}}\\,\\,\\,=m∠A+m∠C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we can solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{{180}^{∘}-m∠B}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Exterior angle at [math]B[/math]}}\\,\\,\\, & =m∠A+m∠C \\\\ {73}^{∘} & ={41}^{∘}+2x \\\\ {32}^{∘} & =2x \\\\ {16}^{∘} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186110401 1074 "Since the exterior angles of a triangle sum to $360^{\\circ}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n145^{∘} + x + (2x - 25^{∘}) &= 360^{∘} \\\\\n120^{∘} + 3x &= 360^{∘} \\\\\n3x &= 240^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 80^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186110401 1074 "Since the exterior angles of a triangle sum to $360^{\\circ}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{145}^{∘}+x+(2x-{25}^{∘}) & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {120}^{∘}+3x & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & ={240}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={80}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186110402 1071 "Given a triangle $\\triangle ABC$, let the exterior angles at ${A}$ and ${B}$ be equal $120^\\circ$ and $100^\\circ$, respectively. What is the measure of the angle $\\angle C$?" 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186110402 1071 "Given a triangle $\\triangle ABC$, let the exterior angles at ${A}$ and ${B}$ be equal $120^\\circ$ and $100^\\circ,$ respectively. What is the measure of the angle $\\angle C?$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186110402 1074 "Let's start by drawing the corresponding triangle.\n\n![](images/3e/3e2698fe8e2e886127669b1e04f45499706750312b447ff0c73524f2de4ff2ce.png)\n\n  \n\nLet $z$ be the measure of the exterior angle at ${C}$ pictured above. Since the sum of the exterior angles of a triangle is $360^{\\circ}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{120}^{∘}+{100}^{∘}+z & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {220}^{∘}+z & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ z & ={140}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the value of the missing interior angle at $C\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠C & ={180}^{∘}-z \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{140}^{∘} \\\\  & ={40}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $m\\angle C = 40^\\circ.$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186110402 1074 "Let's start by drawing the corresponding triangle.\n\n![](images/3e/3e2698fe8e2e886127669b1e04f45499706750312b447ff0c73524f2de4ff2ce.png)\n\nLet $z$ be the measure of the exterior angle at ${C}$ pictured above. Since the sum of the exterior angles of a triangle is $360^{\\circ}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n120^{∘} + 100^{∘} + z &= 360^{∘} \\\\\n220^{∘} + z &= 360^{∘} \\\\\nz &= 140^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the value of the missing interior angle at $C$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠C &= 180^{∘} - z \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} - 140^{∘} \\\\\n&= 40^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nm\\angle C = 40^\\circ\n$$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186110771 1038 17592186505768 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186110771 1038 17592186505791 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186110772 1038 17592186505761 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186110772 1038 17592186505819 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186110773 1038 17592186505783 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186110773 1038 17592186505838 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186110830 1050 540.0 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186111843 1064 "Consider the following triangle, where the side ![](images/72/721aa9559f1b9f3da3e69b1b9763327e29a6f3fb77157f4b42dd076d3865e098.svg) has been extended beyond the vertex ![](images/27/278f99df1e14fe0ebfb61bdb72262a62670dcaa60d322148bc3ee591b278ec2d.svg)\n\n![](images/6b/6be676cc487d17815701f3783e9eca71d7491a0e63d7ae9016fb65ad801e61d6.png)\n\n  \n\nThe resulting angle ![](images/f1/f1729f131b4ec04b45c992a99498753dd09d27e76e917dff66d1fd554ec2ab5e.svg) is called the **exterior angle** at vertex ![](images/27/278f99df1e14fe0ebfb61bdb72262a62670dcaa60d322148bc3ee591b278ec2d.svg) \n\nExterior angles have the following properties:\n\n- An exterior angle and its corresponding interior angle form a linear pair and are therefore supplementary. Here, we have\n![](images/9a/9ae2a4aa323b941eff72a50f5c1086c72e961d0eed919a327306e77cfd5a7ef6.svg)\n\n- The measure of an exterior angle is equal to the sum of the measures of the two non-adjacent interior angles. This is called the **exterior angles theorem**. To see why it is true, notice that since the internal angles of a triangle sum to ![](images/1a/1ace39b6f52fbf0b8654fc88bfa3e97366d33e3c411580da106e513a498aca53.svg) we have \n![](images/49/49aafbdb975ccd101e16c921c20630af6296b860438ebfb62d8a205cc6e7b3c7.svg)\n\n- At each vertex of a triangle, there are two exterior angles. They are vertical angles, and consequently, they have the same measure.\n\n![](images/b4/b459ad1edd5e0fdf512d722eb55ab20ff66c281d7160077d12936a6926529aa0.png)" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186111843 1064 "Consider the following triangle, where the side $\\overline{AC}$ has been extended beyond the vertex $C$.\n\n![](images/6b/6be676cc487d17815701f3783e9eca71d7491a0e63d7ae9016fb65ad801e61d6.png)\n\nThe resulting angle $\\angle{BCD}$ is called the **exterior angle** at vertex $C$.\n\nExterior angles have the following properties:\n\n- An exterior angle and its corresponding interior angle form a linear pair and are therefore supplementary. Here, we have\n$\\underbrace{\\color{YellowOrange}m\\angle{BCD}}_{\\large\\text{Exterior}} \\:+\\: \\underbrace{\\color{blue}m\\angle{ACB}}_{\\large\\text{Interior}} = 180^\\circ$\n- The measure of an exterior angle is equal to the sum of the measures of the two non-adjacent interior angles. This is called the **exterior angles theorem**. To see why it is true, notice that since the internal angles of a triangle sum to $180^\\circ$, we have\n$m∠A + m∠B + m∠C|= 180^{∘}; m∠A + m∠C|= 180^{∘} - m∠B_(⏟)_(Exterior angle at [math]B[/math])$.\n- At each vertex of a triangle, there are two exterior angles. They are vertical angles, and consequently they have the same measure.![](images/b4/b459ad1edd5e0fdf512d722eb55ab20ff66c281d7160077d12936a6926529aa0.png)" 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186111844 1064 "Yet another important fact about exterior angles is:\n\n> The sum of the measures of the exterior angles of a triangle is always equal to $360^\\circ$.\n\nSo, for the triangle below, we have\n\n$$\nx+y+z = 360^\\circ\n$$\n\n![](images/22/22586791e1118a38a554deffaec3ac5b73eafc47eee4be2052c0949dde584cea.png)\n\nTo see why, first recall that the measure of an exterior angle is equal to the sum of the measures of the two non-adjacent interior angles. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= m∠B + m∠C \\\\\ny &= m∠A + m∠C \\\\\nz &= m∠A + m∠B\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, remember that the internal angles of a triangle sum to $180^\\circ$. Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + y + z &= (m∠B + m∠C) + (m∠A + m∠C) + (m∠A + m∠B) \\\\\n&= 2m∠A + 2m∠B + 2m∠C \\\\\n&= 2(m∠A + m∠B + m∠C) \\\\\n&= 2(180^{∘}) \\\\\n&= 360^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186111844 1064 "Yet another important fact about exterior angles is:\n\nThe sum of the measures of the exterior angles of a triangle is always equal to $360^\\circ.$\n\nSo, for the triangle below, we have \n\n$$\nx+y+z = 360^\\circ.\n$$\n\n![](images/22/22586791e1118a38a554deffaec3ac5b73eafc47eee4be2052c0949dde584cea.png)\n\nTo see why, first recall that the measure of an exterior angle is equal to the sum of the measures of the two non-adjacent interior angles. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =m∠B+m∠C \\\\ y & =m∠A+m∠C \\\\ z & =m∠A+m∠B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, remember that the internal angles of a triangle sum to $180^\\circ.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =(m∠B+m∠C)+(m∠A+m∠C)+(m∠A+m∠B) \\\\  & =2m∠A+2m∠B+2m∠C \\\\  & =2(m∠A+m∠B+m∠C) \\\\  & =2({180}^{∘}) \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186138368 1038 17592186505774 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186147415 1038 17592186505807 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186161988 1038 17592186505739 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186161988 1038 17592186505766 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186168807 1075 17592186168808 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186168807 1075 17592186168809 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186168807 1075 17592186168810 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186168807 1075 17592186168811 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186168807 1075 17592186168812 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186168807 1075 17592186505270 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186168807 1075 17592186505340 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186168807 1075 17592186505405 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186168807 1075 17592186505418 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186168807 1075 17592186505421 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186168807 1078 17592186505270 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186168813 1075 17592186168814 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186168813 1075 17592186168815 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186168813 1075 17592186168816 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186168813 1075 17592186168817 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186168813 1075 17592186168818 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186168813 1075 17592186505266 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186168813 1075 17592186505306 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186168813 1075 17592186505422 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186168813 1075 17592186505480 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186168813 1075 17592186505510 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186168813 1078 17592186505510 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186171681 1071 "![](images/b5/b5b4d260ad31b6c4efc3f7314e98df8b422f8d92f0f21fa436334c4b15d88a67.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=100$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\widehat{\\,p}=0.64$, find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 1.645) = 0.05$, where $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, as shown in the diagram above.*" 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186171681 1071 "![](images/b5/b5b4d260ad31b6c4efc3f7314e98df8b422f8d92f0f21fa436334c4b15d88a67.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=100$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\widehat{\\,p}=0.64,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 1.645) = 0.05,$ where $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186171681 1074 "Since $n=100$ and\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}=0.64\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n- $n \\widehat{\\,p} = 100 \\cdot 0.64 = 64 > 5$, and\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p}) = 100 \\cdot (1-0.64) = 36 > 5$.\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p} - p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}\\phantom{\\mid}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nand $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nP(Z > 1.645) = 0.05\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.05} = 1.645\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n0.64 ± 1.645 \\cdot \\sqrt{(0.64(1 - 0.64))/(100)}; 0.64 ± 0.079\n$$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186171681 1074 "Since $n=100$ and $\\widehat{\\,p}=0.64,$ we have that\n\n- $n \\widehat{\\,p} = 100 \\cdot 0.64 = 64 > 5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p}) = 100 \\cdot (1-0.64) = 36 > 5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p} - p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}\\phantom{|}} \\sim N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z > 1.645) = 0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.05} = 1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.64\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.64(1-0.64)}{100}} \\\\  & 0.64\\pm 0.079\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186171682 1071 "A survey of a random sample of $1\\,500$ U.S. households reveals that $780$ of them own at least one dog. Find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the proportion of all U.S. households that own at least one dog.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_p$ such that $P(Z > z_p) = p$ for some particular values of $p$.*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186171682 1071 "A survey of a random sample of $1\\,500$ U.S. households reveals that $780$ of them own at least one dog. Find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the proportion of all U.S. households that own at least one dog.   \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_p$ such that $P(Z > z_p) = p$ for some particular values of $p.$* \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186171682 1074 "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all U.S. households,\n\n- some of the households surveyed own at least one dog, and some don't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=1\\, 500,$ and\n\n- the proportion of households in the sample that own at least one dog can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{p\\,} = \\dfrac{780}{1\\, 500} = 0.52\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n \\widehat{\\,p} =1\\,500 \\cdot 0.52 = 780 > 5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p}) = 1\\,500 \\cdot (1-0.52) = 720 > 5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p} - p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}\\phantom{|}} \\sim N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ 