 | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186172325 1074 "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of the students at a particular university,\n\n- some of the students select at least one math course, and some don't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=150,$ and\n\n- the proportion of students from the sample who select at least one math course can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{p\\,}=\\frac{24}{150}=0.16\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=150\\cdot 0.16=24>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=150\\cdot (1-0.16)=126>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f58f278f66244732c379f3ad0f064227298915b8e7c364b91b8766bb8789c1ea.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.96:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.16\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.16(1-0.16)}{150}} \\\\  & 0.16\\pm 0.059\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186173212 1064 "Let's now discuss how to construct a confidence interval for the proportion of the population whose members have a particular characteristic.\n\nAs a concrete example, let $p$ represent the proportion of the United States voting population that will vote for candidate $A$ in the next presidential election.\n\nSuppose we conduct a random sample of size $n=40$ from our population. After processing our results, we find that the proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ of sample members that plan to vote for candidate $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} = 0.3\n$$\n\nThis is our *estimate* of the population proportion $p$.\n\nOf course, the quantity that we're *really* interested in is $p$, the proportion of the entire voting population that will vote for candidate $A$. We want to get an idea of how accurate our estimate is. Therefore, we wish to construct a confidence interval of the form\n\n$$\n(\\widehat{\\,p}-E, \\: \\widehat{\\,p}+E)\n$$\n\nwhere $E$ is some **margin of error.**\n\nLet's now discuss how to construct a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$." 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186173212 1064 "Let's now discuss how to construct a confidence interval for the proportion of the population whose members have a particular characteristic. \n\nAs a concrete example, let $p$ represent the proportion of the United States voting population that will vote for candidate $A$ in the next presidential election. \n\nSuppose we conduct a random sample of size $n=40$ from our population. After processing our results, we find that the proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ of sample members that plan to vote for candidate $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} = 0.3.\n$$\n\nThis is our *estimate* of the population proportion $p.$ \n\nOf course, the quantity that we're *really* interested in is $p,$ the proportion of the entire voting population that will vote for candidate $A.$ We want to get an idea of how accurate our estimate is. Therefore, we wish to construct a confidence interval of the form\n\n$$\n(\\widehat{\\,p}-E, \\: \\widehat{\\,p}+E),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $E$ is some **margin of error.**\n\nLet's now discuss how to construct a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p.$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186173213 1064 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a sufficiently large population with sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$. Let's also assume that the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- $n \\widehat{\\,p} > 5$\n- $n (1-\\widehat{\\,p}) > 5$\n\nThen, for a given value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\\widehat{\\,p} - z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\\: \\widehat{\\,p} + z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}} \\right)\n$$\n\nThe endpoints of our confidence interval are the **confidence limits** and are given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}\n$$\n\nEach part of the formula above has a name:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion\n- $p$ is the population proportion\n- $n$ is the sample size\n- $z_{\\alpha/2}$ is the corresponding $z$-score\n- $E = z_{\\alpha/2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}$ is the margin of error\n\n$$\nz\n$$\n\nSo, our confidence interval can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n(p^(^) - [margin of error],\\begin{vmatrix}p^(^) + [margin of error]) \\\\ (p^(^) - [[math]z[/math]-score] \\cdot [standard error], \\mid p^(^) + [[math]z[/math]-score] \\cdot [standard error]) \\\\ (p^(^) - z_{α/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{(p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(n)},\\end{vmatrix}p^(^) + z_{α/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{(p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(n)})\n$$\n\nSince computing a confidence interval amounts to finding the corresponding confidence limits, we will use the two terms interchangeably.\n\nFinally, in the future, you may assume that if a population is \"large,\" it is sufficiently larger than any sample drawn from it." 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186173213 1064 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a sufficiently large population with sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}.$ Let's also assume that the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- $n \\widehat{\\,p} > 5$\n\n- $n (1-\\widehat{\\,p}) > 5$\n\nThen, for a given value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\n\\widehat{\\,p} - z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\\:\n\n\\widehat{\\,p} + z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}\n\\right).\n$$\n\nThe endpoints of our confidence interval are the **confidence limits** and are given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}.\n$$\n\nEach part of the formula above has a name:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion\n\n- $n$ is the sample size\n\n- $z_{\\alpha/2}$ is the corresponding $z$-score\n\n- $E = z_{\\alpha/2}\\cdot  \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}$ is the margin of error\n\n![](images/d0/d07c13f30dc4a0960b70f2705bb615f79c0c7ef3a6ed5d7a8eef2401db02d6c2.svg)\n\nSo, our confidence interval can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\overset{^}{\\,p}-[\\text{margin of error}], & \\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}+[\\text{margin of error}]) \\\\ (\\overset{^}{\\,p}-[\\text{[math]z[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}], & \\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}+[\\text{[math]z[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}]) \\\\ (\\overset{^}{\\,p}-{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}}, & \\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}+{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince computing a confidence interval amounts to finding the corresponding confidence limits, we will use the two terms interchangeably.\n\nFinally, in the future, you may assume that if a population is \"large,\" it is sufficiently larger than any sample drawn from it." 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186173223 1064 "We have\n\n$$\nn=40, \\widehat{\\,p} = 0.3\n$$\n\nand we wish to construct a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$.\n\nTo construct our confidence interval, the first thing to do is to establish the sampling distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$.\n\nSince the United States has around $240$ million registered voters, the sample size $n=40$ is clearly less than $10\\%$ of the population size. This means that, provided certain conditions are met, the following approximation can be applied:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left(p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n} \\right)\n$$\n\nLet's check that our usual conditions hold in this case (where we use $\\widehat{\\,p}$ as an estimate for $p$):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nnp^(^) &= 40 \\cdot 0.3 \\\\\n&= 12 \\\\\n&> 5✓ \\\\\nn(1 - p^(^)) &= 40 \\cdot (1 - 0.3) \\\\\n&= 28 \\\\\n&> 5✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince all three conditions hold, the above approximation is applicable.\n\nBy transforming $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable, we have\n\n$$\nZ= \\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p} - p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}\\phantom{\\mid}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nWe wish to find a $z$-interval that we're $95\\%$ confident that the random variable $Z$ lies within. This interval is indicated in the diagram below:\n\n![](images/50/50a0f49fe5c96ba3259832fbf1a0abcedffb342301ca069bb7b88d157a181019.png)\n\nTo help with the notation, we define a new parameter $\\alpha$. Since we're computing a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nis precisely the area bounded by each \"tail\" that we're excluding from our confidence interval. Let's label the critical values at the endpoints of our interval as $\\pm z_{\\alpha/2}$:\n\n![](images/3c/3cdfd2c72bf7fe65e5d1b584d46d4c846e74615e54c739a6d1ea990c95011a8a.png)\n\nAccording to our diagram,\n\n$$\nP(Z > z_{0.025}) = P(Z < -z_{0.025}) = 0.025\n$$\n\nUsing a percentage points table for the standard normal distribution, we find that\n\n$$\nz_{0.025} \\approx 1.96\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Z > 1.96) = P(Z < -1.96) = 0.025\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\nP(-1.96 < Z < 1.96) = 0.95\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is a $95\\%$ probability that our random variable $Z$ will lie in the interval $(-1.96, 1.96)$.\n\nNow, here's the trick. We solve the inequality inside the parentheses of the last probability statement for the population proportion $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1.96 & < Z < 1.96 \\\\ -1.96 & < (p^(^) - p)/(\\sqrt{p^(^)(1 - p^(^))/n}) < 1.96 \\\\ -1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{(p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(n)} & < p^(^) - p < 1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{(p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(n)} \\\\ -p^(^) - 1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{(p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(n)} & < - p < - p^(^) + 1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{(p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(n)} \\\\ p^(^) + 1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{(p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(n)} & > p > p^(^) - 1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{(p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(n)} \\\\ p^(^) - 1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{(p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(n)} & < p < p^(^) + 1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{(p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(n)}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion ${p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\\widehat{\\,p} -1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\\: \\widehat{\\,p} +1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}} \\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting our values\n\n$$\nn=40, \\qquad \\widehat{\\,p}=0.3\n$$\n\nwe obtain that our $95\\%$ confidence interval for ${p}$ is\n\n$$\n\\big(0.3 - 0.142, \\: 0.3 + 0.142 \\big)\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\big(0.158, \\: 0.442 \\big)\n$$\n\nNow that we've seen how to construct a particular confidence interval, let's discuss the more general procedure." 13194139535455 17592186045417 false] [17592186173223 1064 "We have ![](images/4a/4a616e999005a19046f642d63f02ed9d7054edec758f6a03edb7eb90c29e8713.svg) and we wish to construct a ![](images/ad/ad3179b1ff9f2cbef8f6af088f855b2d2b04b97f3e533621283cfdc90bc30ada.svg) confidence interval for the population proportion ![](images/1d/1d3e45623fb292952d8c207690797505cc7c484a6410f4a95aff94a51b4a0ee5.svg)\n\nTo construct our confidence interval, the first thing to do is to establish the sampling distribution of ![](images/be/be3d0efacea6b66b85bae982c4d923f2473fe722d5f30c4b9a940ac67773cff2.svg)\n\nSince the United States has around ![](images/59/592c5e42c05234443b91e6d53afea4f21fb7e5dfb0ab1e645fce7ffe881bfc9c.svg) million registered voters, the sample size ![](images/35/3597ee702449dc99d1b986728db87df4b1e6ee3f2ff01bf7db08ad6c48a9bd42.svg) is clearly less than ![](images/41/414c7a992cb07b0cf60bef2446dc3eb4072aeb99acedf6d77c02b5c183cdfc63.svg) of the population size. This means that, provided certain conditions are met, the following approximation can be applied:\n\n![](images/24/24bc598b02901990c48811d40a22934c7f4d59be5e57bcb7f8ac6ca592d1abcb.svg)\n\nLet's check that our usual conditions hold in this case (where we use ![](images/0e/0e92bef16c8ef7c460a72dcba19c450d46eb09079ba887a47987d8af51df613f.svg) as an estimate for ![](images/48/48c7b594e8628a17b991d4e65e7bee6ffe6680dd410f036cfae83154bbd55fc6.svg)):\n\n![](images/bf/bfd75db155b00910cb00aa4c37df692c254df765ec94ba265af589f72868071d.svg)\n\nSince all three conditions hold, the above approximation is applicable. \n\nBy transforming ![](images/0e/0e92bef16c8ef7c460a72dcba19c450d46eb09079ba887a47987d8af51df613f.svg) to a standard normal random variable, we have\n\n![](images/c4